angelv.clock
============

This work is based on Omarchy. It is a derivative work of the Omarchy
shell's built-in clock widget (omarchy.clock), used under the MIT License.
The upstream copyright notice is retained in LICENSE alongside the
copyright covering the modifications made here.

Specifically, it began as a byte-for-byte clone of the Omarchy shell's
first-party clock bar widget (`omarchy.clock`), created with:

    omarchy plugin clone omarchy.clock

Upstream project : Omarchy — https://github.com/basecamp/omarchy
Upstream author  : David Heinemeier Hansson / Basecamp
Upstream license : MIT

The files BarWidget.qml, Model.js and Panel.qml are derived from that
widget and retain substantial portions of the original code. See LICENSE
for the full terms and the retained copyright notice.

Changes made in this derivative:

  * Made the "memento mori" life bar optional, behind a `mementoMori`
    setting. It remains on by default, as upstream.
  * Added a time-zone converter section beneath the calendar: per-zone
    sun/moon glyphs, a 24-hour day/night rail, and a magnetic scrubber.
  * Added read-only calendar sync (see the second derivation below).


Second derivation: sync-calendar-omarchy
----------------------------------------

The calendar sync added in this plugin is derived from sync-calendar-omarchy,
used under the MIT License.

Upstream project : sync-calendar-omarchy
                   https://github.com/promaaa/sync-calendar-omarchy
Upstream author  : promaaa
Upstream license : MIT

`fetch-events.py` retains substantial portions of that project's
`fetch-events.py`: the iCalendar reader (line unfolding, text unescaping,
TZID resolution and datetime parsing), the RRULE expanders for daily,
weekly, monthly and yearly recurrences, multi-day event expansion, the
meeting-URL detection, and the feed fetcher.

Removed from that file in this derivative: the Google Calendar API and its
OAuth helper, all JMAP support, the local offline calendar, every event
create and delete path, and the machine-translation of event titles. What
remains is read-only .ics / webcal fetching, which is all that Google's
"secret address in iCal format" and Apple's published calendar links need.

In Model.js, `parseEventsFile`, `formatSelectedDateLabel` and
`formatAgendaMarkdown` are adapted from that project's Model.js.

